          <control type="list" format="string" />
        </setting>
        <!-- <dependencies>/<dependency type="enable" setting="...">
             is the pattern already proven working in this exact file
             (see verify_ssl above, confirmed live greyed out correctly).
             Two earlier attempts at the inline eq()-attribute form
             (both plausible-looking, sourced from reading Kodi's own
             AddonSettings.cpp) did not actually render as disabled when
             tested live, for reasons not fully root-caused. Trusting the
             pattern already confirmed live in this codebase over what
             the C++ source alone suggested. Only meaningful when the
             timezone dropdown above is "Manual" (see
             EffectiveRecurringRuleUtcOffsetMinutes()'s own comment). -->
        <setting id="recurring_rule_utc_offset_minutes" type="integer" label="30049" help="30050">
          <level>1</level>
          <default>0</default>
          <constraints>
            <minimum>-720</minimum>
            <maximum>840</maximum>
          </constraints>
          <control type="edit" format="integer" />
          <dependencies>
            <dependency type="enable" setting="recurring_rule_timezone">manual</dependency>
          </dependencies>
        </setting>
        <!-- Visible-but-permanently-disabled, same pattern (and same reason)
             as dispatcharr_timezone_info above: read-only reference so the
             greyed-out padding fields right below are self-explanatory
             instead of a silent mystery: placed directly above them, not
             next to the unrelated timezone settings, for exactly that
             reason. <control><hidden>true</hidden></control> (the password
             field's pattern) only masks a text control's displayed value
             and does nothing useful on a toggle control (confirmed live,
             2026-09-09); not used here. A first version instead used
             visible="false" as an attribute on <setting> itself, on the
             reasoning that it's addon-managed state a user shouldn't hand-
             toggle; reconsidered: <enable>false</enable> already prevents
             that (same as the timezone reference field), without also
             hiding the one piece of context that explains why the padding
             fields below are disabled. That reasoning happened to be right
             for an unrelated reason found later (2026-09-27, a 46th-pass
             audit, confirmed against Kodi's own real current source): the
             attribute form was never a real, working mechanism to begin
             with: this file's own <settings version="1"> root parses
             visibility only via a <visible> child element
             (ISetting::Deserialize(), settings/lib/ISetting.cpp), and
             silently ignores the attribute form entirely (only the legacy
             version="0" definitions parser reads it). The api_key_host/
             api_key_username settings below made exactly this mistake for
             real and had to be corrected; see their own comment. -->
        <setting id="dispatcharr_is_admin" type="boolean" label="30117" help="30118">
          <level>1</level>
          <default>true</default>
          <control type="toggle" />
          <enable>false</enable>
        </setting>
        <!-- Gated on dispatcharr_is_admin above, same proven <dependencies>
             pattern as recurring_rule_utc_offset_minutes further up (see
             that setting's own comment): Dispatcharr itself rejects a
             write to these from a non-admin account (confirmed against its
             real source, CoreSettingsViewSet's update action requires
             IsAdmin), so a non-admin account can read the current padding
             here but not usefully change it. -->
        <setting id="recording_pre_offset_minutes" type="integer" label="30054" help="30055">
          <level>0</level>
          <default>0</default>
          <constraints>
            <minimum>0</minimum>
            <!-- Raised from 120 (a 27th-pass audit, 2026-09-26): Dispatcharr's
                 own DVR settings form sets no maximum at all (confirmed
                 against its real frontend source), so a real server-side
                 value above this addon's own old, lower cap made Kodi's own
                 SetSettingInt() a silent no-op (CSettingInt::CheckValidity()
                 rejects an out-of-range value, confirmed against Kodi's real
                 source), with real, confirmed consequences, see
                 PVRDispatcharr.cpp's own constructor comment on this exact
                 setting. 1440 (24h) comfortably covers any real padding use
                 case (e.g. a multi-hour sports-overrun post-padding value)
                 while still bounding the field to something sane. -->
            <maximum>1440</maximum>
          </constraints>
          <control type="edit" format="integer" />
          <dependencies>
            <dependency type="enable" setting="dispatcharr_is_admin">true</dependency>
          </dependencies>
        </setting>
        <setting id="recording_post_offset_minutes" type="integer" label="30056" help="30057">
          <level>0</level>
          <default>0</default>
          <constraints>
            <minimum>0</minimum>
            <!-- Same 27th-pass fix as recording_pre_offset_minutes above. -->
            <maximum>1440</maximum>
          </constraints>
          <control type="edit" format="integer" />
          <dependencies>
            <dependency type="enable" setting="dispatcharr_is_admin">true</dependency>
          </dependencies>
        </setting>
      </group>
    </category>

    <category id="advanced" label="30030">
      <group id="1">
        <setting id="debug_logging" type="boolean" label="30031" help="30032">
          <level>2</level>
          <default>false</default>
          <control type="toggle" />
        </setting>
        <setting id="api_key" type="string" label="30033" help="30034">
          <level>2</level>
          <default></default>
          <constraints>
            <allowempty>true</allowempty>
          </constraints>
          <control type="edit" format="string" />
        </setting>
        <!-- Hidden addon-managed state, not user-facing: which
             server/username api_key above was actually generated for (the
             stored value is scheme://host:port, not a bare host, since a
             48th-pass audit folded port and scheme into it too; the
             setting id itself stayed api_key_host unchanged), so the
             constructor can tell a stored key apart from a *different*
             account/server's key that happens to still be sitting in
             settings.xml. Added specifically because OnAddonSettingChanged()
             itself must never call SetSetting*() synchronously (a real,
             confirmed GUI-thread deadlock otherwise; see
             PVRDispatcharr.cpp's own comment on why the host/username
             branch no longer clears api_key directly): Kodi's own
             TransferSettings() re-delivers every setting on the very same
             call stack a settings-dialog save already re-enters through,
             so persisting host/username here at the same time as api_key
             (both from PersistApiKeyIfChanged(), never from inside a live
             settings-change callback) and having the constructor compare
             them is what actually detects a stale key now, not a
             synchronous clear at change time. Uses a real <visible> child
             element, not a `visible="false"` attribute (fixed 2026-09-27,
             a 46th-pass audit, correcting a real, confirmed mistake in
             this same fix one pass earlier, confirmed against Kodi's own
             real current source): this file's own <settings version="1">
             root routes through CAddonSettings::InitializeDefinitionsFromXml()
             (AddonSettings.cpp), whose ISetting::Deserialize()
             (settings/lib/ISetting.cpp) reads visibility only via
             XMLUtils::GetBoolean(node, "visible", ...): FirstChild("visible"),
             a child element, never an attribute on <setting> itself. The
             attribute form is silently ignored by this parser (it's only
             ever read by ParseOldSettingElement(), the legacy
             version="0" definitions this file doesn't use), so both
             settings below rendered as two extra, fully visible, editable
             "API key" rows (reusing label 30033) showing the stored
             host/username in plain text, the opposite of the intent.
             (Corrected 2026-09-27, a 56th-pass audit: this comment
             previously said the dispatcharr_is_admin setting further up
             "cites this same wrong attribute-form claim in its own older
             comment": that was itself stale by the time it was written;
             that setting's own comment had already been corrected to say
             the attribute form "was never a real, working mechanism to
             begin with", not the wrong claim this comment described.) -->
        <setting id="api_key_host" type="string" label="30033" help="30034">
          <level>2</level>
          <default></default>
          <constraints>
            <allowempty>true</allowempty>
          </constraints>
          <control type="edit" format="string" />
          <visible>false</visible>
        </setting>
        <setting id="api_key_username" type="string" label="30033" help="30034">
          <level>2</level>
          <default></default>
          <constraints>
            <allowempty>true</allowempty>
          </constraints>
          <control type="edit" format="string" />
          <visible>false</visible>
        </setting>
      </group>
    </category>

  </section>
</settings>
